ne.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{y-{y}_{a}}{{y}_{b}-{y}_{a}} & =\\frac{x-{x}_{a}}{{x}_{b}-{x}_{a}} \\\\ \\frac{y-0}{5-0} & =\\frac{x-0}{6-0} \\\\ \\frac{y}{5} & =\\frac{x}{6} \\\\ y & =\\frac{5}{6}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $c=⟨{c}_{1},{c}_{2}⟩$ satisfies the equation of the line, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{c}_{2} & =\\frac{5}{6}{c}_{1}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, taking into account that ${c}_{2}=\\frac{\\sqrt{26}}{2},$ we obtain that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\sqrt{26}}{2} & =\\frac{5}{6}{c}_{1} \\\\ {c}_{1} & =\\frac{6}{5}\\cdot \\frac{\\sqrt{26}}{2} \\\\ {c}_{1} & =\\frac{3\\sqrt{26}}{5}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the point $C$ that satisfies the mean value theorem is $$\n\\displaystyle (\\frac{3\\sqrt{26}}{5},\\frac{\\sqrt{26}}{2}).\n$$" 13194139539078 17592186576411 true] [17592186363059 1074 "The mean value theorem states that if $f(x,y)$ is differentiable at each point on the line segment $\\overset{―}{AB},$ then there exists a point $C\\in \\overset{―}{AB}$ with position vector $c$ such that\n\n$$\nf(b)-f(a)=∇f(c)·(b-a).\n$$\n\nNotice that the function $f(x,y)=\\sqrt{169-{y}^{2}}$ is differentiable at each point on the line segment $\\overset{―}{AB}.$ So, we can apply the mean value theorem.\n\nSubstituting $a=⟨0,0⟩$ and $b=⟨6,5⟩$ into the above expression, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(6,5)-f(0,0) & =∇f(c)\\cdot (⟨6,5⟩-⟨0,0⟩) \\\\ \\sqrt{169-{5}^{2}}-\\sqrt{169-{0}^{2}} & =∇f(c)\\cdot ⟨6,5⟩ \\\\ 12-13 & =∇f(c)\\cdot ⟨6,5⟩ \\\\ -1 & =∇f(c)\\cdot ⟨6,5⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nComputing $∇f$ and evaluating at $c=⟨{c}_{1},{c}_{2}⟩,$ we get \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f & =⟨0,-\\frac{y}{\\sqrt{169-{y}^{2}}}⟩, \\\\ ∇f(c) & =⟨0,\\,-\\frac{{c}_{2}}{\\sqrt{169-{{c}_{2}}^{2}}}⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-1 & =∇f⟨c⟩\\cdot ⟨6,5⟩ \\\\ -1 & =⟨0,\\,-\\frac{{c}_{2}}{\\sqrt{169-{{c}_{2}}^{2}}}⟩\\cdot ⟨6,5⟩ \\\\ -1 & =-\\frac{5{c}_{2}}{\\sqrt{169-{{c}_{2}}^{2}}} \\\\ \\sqrt{169-{{c}_{2}}^{2}} & =5{c}_{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\