2186576411 true] [17592186369511 1071 "![](images/e6/e6252037001e1a5548ede86a16b4ddde8f5aec8fd956bded55a3506f7b533dbe.png)\n\nCalculate ${∮}_{∂D}(\\frac{1}{3}{y}^{3}+\\operatorname{cos}⁡x)\\,dx+(\\operatorname{ln}⁡({sin}^{2}⁡y)-\\frac{1}{3}{x}^{3})\\,dy,$ where $D$ is the annular region shown above." 13194139539078 17592186576411 false] [17592186369518 1071 "![](images/b2/b232a4cddf05a425709c1b3204b69b0126e586b42a6af73b867dd75ee294d961.png)\n\nCalculate \n$$\n\\displaystyle {∮}_{∂D}({x}^{3}{y}^{3}+x)\\,dx+({x}^{4}{y}^{2}-y)\\,dy,\n$$\nwhere $D$ is the region shown above." 13194139539078 17592186576411 true] [17592186369518 1071 "![](images/b2/b232a4cddf05a425709c1b3204b69b0126e586b42a6af73b867dd75ee294d961.png)\n\nCalculate \n${∮}_{∂D}({x}^{3}{y}^{3}+x)\\,dx+({x}^{4}{y}^{2}-y)\\,dy,$\nwhere $D$ is the region shown above." 13194139539078 17592186576411 false] [17592186369525 1071 "![](images/75/754aa1ded10b20362350f3da9e5b4dbe5f9f194c2289af348ff9de34f2fb8cc5.png)\n\nCalculate \n$$\n\\displaystyle {∮}_{∂D}(x{y}^{2}+{x}^{3})\\,dx+(2{x}^{2}y-{y}^{3})\\,dy,\n$$\nwhere $D$ is the region shown above." 13194139539078 17592186576411 true] [17592186369525 1071 "![](images/75/754aa1ded10b20362350f3da9e5b4dbe5f9f194c2289af348ff9de34f2fb8cc5.png)\n\nCalculate \n${∮}_{∂D}(x{y}^{2}+{x}^{3})\\,dx+(2{x}^{2}y-{y}^{3})\\,dy,$\nwhere $D$ is the region shown above." 13194139539078 17592186576411 false] [17592186369539 1074 "Let $p$ represent the profit on a single roll of the game. On each roll in the game, there are two possible outcomes:\n\n- If the sum of the outcomes is $6$ ($(1,5),(2,4),(3,3),(4,2)$, or $(5,1)$), the player wins $$6,$ so we have $p=6.$\n\n- If the sum of the outcomes is not $6,$ the player loses $$1,$ so we have $p=-1.$\n\nThe probabilities for the outcomes are\n\n$$\nP(6)=\\frac{5}{36},\\,P(-1)=\\frac{31}{36}.\n$$\n\nLet's summarize this in a table. If $p$ is the profit (in dollars) on a single game, and $f(p)$ is the probability mass function for $p,$ then we have the following:\n\n| $f(p)$ | $\\frac{5}{36}$ | $\\frac{31}{36}$ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $p$ | $6$ | $-1$ |\n\nTherefore, the expected value of $p$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}E[p] & =6\\cdot f(6)+(-1)\\cdot f(-1) \\\\  & =6\\left(\\frac{5}{36}\\right)+(-1)\\left(\\frac{31}{36}\\right) \\\\  & =\\frac{30}{36}-\\frac{31}{36} \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{36} \\\\  & ≈-0.028\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the expected profit is $-$0.028.$" 13194139539078 17592186576411 false] [17592186369539 1074 "Let $p$ represent the profit on a single roll of the game. On each roll in the game, there are two possible outcomes:\n\n- If the sum of the outcomes is $6$ ($(1,5),(2,4),(3,3),(4,2)$, or $(5,1)$), the player wins $$6,$ so we have $p=6.$\n\n- If the sum of the outcomes is not $6,$ the player loses $$1,$ so we have $p=-1.$\n\nThe probabilities for the outcomes are\n\n$$$\n\\displaystyle \nP(6)=\\frac{5}{36},\\,P(-1)=\\frac{31}{36}.\n\n$$$\n\nLet's summarize this in a table. If $p$ is the profit (in dollars) on a single game, and $f(p)$ is the probability mass function for $p,$ then we have the following:\n\n| $f(p)$ | $\\frac{5}{36}$ | $\\frac{31}{36}$ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $p$ | $6$ | $-1$ |\n\nTherefore, the expected value of $p$ is given by\n\n$$$\n\\displaystyle \n\\begin{aligned}E[p] & =6\\cdot f(6)+(-1)\\cdot f(-1) \\\\  & =6\\left(\\frac{5}{36}\\right)+(-1)\\left(\\frac{31}{36}\\right) \\\\  & =\\frac{30}{36}-\\frac{31}{36} \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{36} \\\\  & ≈-0.028\\end{aligned}\n\n$$$\n\nTherefore, the expected profit is $-$0.028.$" 13194139539078 17592186576411 true] [17592186369693 1071 "$$\n\\displaystyle |\\begin{aligned}3 & 2 & -2 & -2 \\\\ 1 & -1 & 0 & 3 \\\\ 2 & 5 & 0 & 1 \\\\ -2 & 4 & 1 & -2\\end{aligned}|=\n$$" 13194139539078 17592186576411 true] [17592186369693 1071 "$|\\begin{aligned}3 & 2 & -2 & -2 \\\\ 1 & -1 & 0 & 3 \\\\ 2 & 5 & 0 & 1 \\\\ -2 & 4 & 1 & -2\\end{aligned}|=$" 13194139539078 17592186576411 false] [17592186369818 1071 "Evaluate the integra