\frac{4}{9{n}^{2}-21}>0$ for any natural $n\\ge 2.$ With that in mind, we have\n\n$$\n|{a}_{n}-L|=|\\frac{4}{9{n}^{2}-21}|=\\frac{4}{9{n}^{2}-21}.\n$$\n\nNow, we solve the inequality $|{a}_{n}-L|<ε$ for $n\\ge 2:$\n\nSolving this inequality for $n,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{4}{9{n}^{2}-21} & <ε \\\\ 4 & <ε(9{n}^{2}-21) \\\\ \\frac{4}{ε} & <9{n}^{2}-21 \\\\ \\frac{4}{ε}+21 & <9{n}^{2} \\\\ \\frac{4}{9ε}+\\frac{7}{3} & <{n}^{2} \\\\ n & >\\sqrt{\\frac{7}{3}+\\frac{4}{9ε}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAs a result, if we want $$\n\\displaystyle |\\frac{1-{n}^{2}}{3{n}^{2}-7}-\\left(-\\frac{1}{3}\\right)|<ε,\n$$ it's sufficient to take all values of $n$ that are larger than $\\sqrt{\\frac{7}{3}+\\frac{4}{9ε}}.$\n\nWe can now continue the proof:\n\nTherefore, we choose $N>\\sqrt{\\frac{7}{3}+\\frac{4}{9ε}}.$\n\nNotice that, since $\\frac{4}{9ε}>0,$ then $$\n\\displaystyle \\sqrt{\\frac{7}{3}+\\frac{4}{9ε}}>\\sqrt{\\frac{7}{3}}≈1.53\n$$ and therefore $N\\ge 2.$\n\nWith that in mind, we'll find the upper bound for the absolute value of the sequence's $n$th term:\n\nThen, for any natural number $n\\ge N,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|\\frac{1-{n}^{2}}{3{n}^{2}-7}-\\left(-\\frac{1}{3}\\right)| & =\\frac{4}{9{n}^{2}-21} \\\\  & <\\frac{4}{9{(\\sqrt{\\frac{7}{3}+\\frac{4}{9ε}})}^{2}-21} \\\\  & =\\frac{4}{(21+\\frac{4}{ε}-21)} \\\\  & =ε.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we make the conclusion:\n\nTherefore, according to the definition, $$\n\\displaystyle {lim}_{n\\to \\infty }\\frac{1-{n}^{2}}{3{n}^{2}-7}=-\\frac{1}{3}.\n$$" 13194139539078 17592186576411 true] [17592186376552 1074 "Recall that\n\n$$\n{lim}_{n\\to \\infty }\\frac{1-{n}^{2}}{3{n}^{2}-7}=-\\frac{1}{3}\n$$\n\nmeans that\n\n$$\n∀ε>0,\\,∃N\\in N,∀n\\in N,\\,n\\ge N\\,⇒\\,|\\frac{1-{n}^{2}}{3{n}^{2}-7}-\\left(-\\frac{1}{3}\\right)|<ε.\n$$\n\nLet's write this in words.\n\nWe need to show that for any real $ε>0,$ there exists a natural number $N$ such that, for every natural number $n\\ge N,$ we have\n\n$$\n|\\fra